      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Note about CFG",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "CFG at 2.0 enables negative prompting which gives better realistic style transfer.\n\nHowever, it also doubles generation times and isn't always necessary, so you might be able to get by setting it to 1.  "
      ],
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    },
    {
      "id": 1195,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        -140,
        -1870
      ],
      "size": [
        430,
        470
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 16,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "About this Workflow",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "The intent of this workflow is to have Krea 2 convert stylized images into a realistic ones while retaining their composition and creative features.\n\nThe reason I put this together is that realism models are inherently not as expressive as stylized ones, and tend to lack diversity in anatomy and faces. But what I found is that by denoising Krea 2 over a stylized image, I could get a realistic-looking image while retaining those features.\n\nThe result is something that tends to look better than what Krea 2 is able to create through prompting alone.\n\n_________\n\nHowever, a difficulty with this approach is that the results depend on how well you can prompt for a realistic styled image that closely matches the input image. And prompting that from scratch is very difficult.  \n\nLuckily Krea 2 uses a text encoder that's also a vision language model, which can be used to both interpret images and build prompts.  \n\nThe typical approach involves using this to generate text for you to paste into the prompt. But this also involves instructing it with an ideal system prompt. And no matter how well I've tried to configure these, I haven't been able to get one that works universally.  \n\nThe approach we take instead with this workflow is to use the [Krea2TexTEncoder](https://github.com/ethanfel/ComfyUI-Krea2TextEncoder) node which instead incorporates its vision directly into the conditioning to avoid the loss that comes from vision -> text.  \n\nThis allows the model to produce a much more accurate recreation of the image. But it also lessens user control, as it will inject its full understanding of the image into the prompt, and you can't see or adjust this.    \n\nWe can tweak its output somewhat with the user prompt, but it's impossible to fully prevent it from injecting stylized features from the image.  \n\nTo get around that I find negative prompting is needed, which requires more than 1 CFG.\n\n\n"
      ],
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    },
